<p>
  Sending is the same four steps every time: pick the document, place the
  fields, name the people, send. This page covers the choices you get along
  the way and what happens after the document leaves you.
</p>

<h2>Pick the document</h2>

<p>
  Upload a PDF or a Word file (.docx or .doc) from the dashboard, or reuse a
  template you have already set up. Word files are converted to PDF for you in
  the background; the builder shows a "Converting Word document&hellip;" card
  and the pages appear by themselves when it is done.
</p>

<h2>Add the people who sign</h2>

<ol>
  <li>Open the document and choose <strong>Send</strong>.</li>
  <li>Type an email address for each signer. The detailed view also lets you add their name, and fill in anything you already know for them.</li>
  <li>If the order matters &mdash; a manager after an employee, say &mdash; switch on the signing order so the second person is only invited once the first has finished.</li>
  <li>Send. Each signer gets their own private link. Nobody can see anyone else's link.</li>
</ol>

<p>
  Two shortcuts sit beside the ordinary send. <strong>Sign it yourself</strong>
  opens the document for you to complete on the spot &mdash; useful for a form
  you fill in in front of someone. A <strong>share link</strong> is one address
  you can publish or paste into a message; everyone who opens it starts their
  own copy.
</p>

<h2>While it is out</h2>

<p>
  The document's page shows each person's state: sent, opened, completed or
  declined. You can resend an invitation, and on the paid plan you can have
  EsignCenter chase people with automatic reminders. If a signer declines, you
  are told, with the reason they gave.
</p>

<h2>Fixing a mistake after somebody has signed</h2>

<p>
  It happens: the document is signed and only then does the wrong figure jump
  out. Use <strong>Resubmit</strong> on the document, correct it, and send the
  same document out again. EsignCenter treats that as the same document rather
  than a new one.
</p>

<aside class="help-callout">
  <p>
    <strong>Good to know:</strong> correcting a document somebody has already
    signed never costs you a second completion, however many rounds of
    corrections it takes &mdash; and it is allowed even when you have reached
    the free plan's monthly completion limit. It does count as one of your
    sends, because a new copy really does go out.
  </p>
</aside>

<h2>When the last person finishes</h2>

<p>
  Everyone receives the completed PDF by email. It carries a digital seal and
  a trusted timestamp, so any later change to the file is detectable, and an
  audit trail PDF lists what happened and when. On the free plan our footer
  appears on the emails we send for you and on the pages your signers sign on;
  the paid plan removes it.
</p>

